  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "The graphs of the linear function \\(y=x+1\\) and the inverse proportion function \\(y=6/x\\) intersect at points with x-coordinates 2 and -3. What is the solution set of the inequality \\(x+1>6/x\\)?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x>2\\)",
    "B": "\\(-3<x<0\\) or \\(x>2\\)",
    "C": "\\(x<-3\\) or \\(0<x<2\\)",
    "D": "\\(-3<x<2\\)"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Since \\(6>0\\), \\(y=6/x\\) decreases on each of \\(x<0\\) and \\(x>0\\), while \\(y=x+1\\) increases. Moving right from an intersection therefore raises the line's value and lowers the inverse proportion function's value.\n\nOn the negative branch, the graphs agree at \\(x=-3\\). The line is above the inverse proportion function for \\(-3<x<0\\) and below it for \\(x<-3\\). On the positive branch, the same comparison at \\(x=2\\) shows that the line is above for \\(x>2\\) and below for \\(0<x<2\\).\n\nThus the required range is \\(-3<x<0\\) or \\(x>2\\). The points \\(x=-3\\) and \\(x=2\\) give equality, and \\(x=0\\) is undefined. The answer is B.",
